## Final status

| Item | Closed by |
|---|---|
| #1 loops bypass success gate | `_trace_succeeded` on Runner; per-attempt traces in both loops |
| #2 `ERROR_PREFIXES` regression | Expanded tuple covers `edit_file`, `shell`, `web_search`, `web_fetch`, `analyze_python`; documented drift rule |
| #3 `"NO OUTPUT"` counted as success | `_step_once` returns `""`; `step` fallback sets `"NO OUTPUT"` *after* the gate; gate rejects both |
| #4 API leaks skills across callers | `cfg.learning.enabled = False` per request |
| `secrets.compare_digest` | `require_key` uses it |
| `_dispatch` mutates args | `safe` copy for the `run_shell` branch |
| `Tracker.finish` leaves `current` set | `self.current = None` on finish |
| `solve_loop` substring `DONE` | `re.search(r"\bDONE\b", ...)` |
| `search_query` in loops | `_step_once` accepts it; loops pass the original order |

**Known limitations, documented in code:**

- `ERROR_PREFIXES` uses `startswith`, so a `read_file` whose *first line* is literally `"ERROR: ..."` marks the trace as failed. This is the tradeoff the reviewer endorsed; the alternative is canonicalizing every tool to raise on failure. The comment in `ERROR_PREFIXES` flags this so the next contributor knows the drift rule.
- The API is single-tenant by construction. Per-user memory and skills need session-keyed storage, out of scope for this release; the docstring in `api.py` says so.

The tree is now mergeable against every blocking item raised across all three reviews.